            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"Consecration, divine appointment, and empowerment by the Holy Spirit for a sacred office or mission.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"Marking and appointing a person or thing to a specific office or work, explicitly distinguishing the act of appointment from the visible sign (oil) and the empowering agent (the Spirit).\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Consecration and spiritual empowerment.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Divine appointment to an office or work.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"SAME_CASES\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book restricts the definition of 'anointing' strictly to the appointment or marking itself, whereas the consensus often blends the appointment, the sign (oil), and the power (the Spirit) into a single overarching concept.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"None; the book clarifies and partitions the elements of the consensus view rather than replacing the central referent of divine appointment.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book agrees that anointing involves consecration to an office but refines the theological definition by conceptually separating the appointment from the empowering presence of the Spirit.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"In 1 John 2:20, 27, believers are said to have an 'anointing' that abides in them and teaches them, which strongly suggests that in some New Testament contexts, the anointing itself is effectively synonymous with the indwelling Spirit, challenging the book's strict separation.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
